A||||4||JC Webb||Does tire condition affect alignment?||||1||
Z||000000||JC Webb||01-17-2001||08:03 PM||||My right rear suspension has way to much positive camber and has worn the outside of the tire significantly.  I plan to align it tommorrow at our school's shop.  Do I need to move the front tires to the rear so that it will align properly?  The front tires are in perfect condition.  My automotive instructor says it makes no difference, but I disagree.<BR>Funny, he says the tire pressure should be checked when an alignment is done, but says tire condition makes no difference!  Any help will be appreciated.  Thanks!<P>John C. Webb||||reg||1
Z||000001||strdream||01-17-2001||08:27 PM||||John<BR>The tire pressure can efect the stance of the car and thus change the angle of the lower control arm. Slight differance in tread wear will not have much efect on tire stance.<BR>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||01-17-2001||09:50 PM||||blank<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 01-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||01-17-2001||09:51 PM||||You should check your tire pressure and correct it before you do an alignment. Rotate the tires also but don't cross rotate them. Keep them on the same side of the car. The tire wear is not going to effect the alignment. I am an ex mechanic.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||01-18-2001||04:43 PM||||your not going to get a good alignment with a tire thats worn on the inside or outside. Two different brands of tires on each side can throw it off too. Tire sizes, even though the numbers are the same, are not standardized betweeen manufacturers.||||reg||1
